WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.540
Speaker 1: It's great to be joined today by
Dr. Shira Doron, who is the chief infection

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:10.280
control officer for Tufts Medicine Health
System, and also the hospital epidemiologist

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.009
at Tufts Medical Center in Boston.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:14.070
Really great having you on today.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.219
I appreciate your time.

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:16.580
Speaker 3: Great to be with you.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:22.850
Speaker 1: So I've been reading all of these
analyzes and meta analyzes and meta meta analyzes

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:29.330
of what might be thought of as a simple question
Do masks work to prevent COVID?

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:37.100
And it seems as though even the most well-intentioned
and sort of science facing people have had

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:42.489
different and sometimes changing perspectives
on this question throughout the pandemic.

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:47.960
There's also sort of a question of what do
we mean by do they work?

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.949
And that might mean what is the effect for
an individual of their decision to wear a

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:59.870
mask versus what is a policy of saying we
require masking in these those or other situations?

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.190
What does that do on the population level?

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.190
Infection rate.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.189
So start wherever you want on this question
and then we can kind of go from there.

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:11.430
Where are you maybe now on this issue?

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.070
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, I think you hit the
nail on the head, right?

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.460
This is not a simple question.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.590
Do masks work?

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:24.460
And you know, where I am now is is in many
ways where I started before the pandemic was

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:29.990
even, you know, a glimmer in our eye, which
is that I have quite literally trusted masks

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:34.020
with my life, my whole career.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.770
I have obviously worn masks in health care.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.939
I'm not a surgeon, so I've worn masks for
my own protection.

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:42.710
And I believe in them.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:49.180
I have taken care of patients with deadly
bacterial meningitis in an ear loop surgical

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.180
mask.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:57.780
I have taken care of patients with H1N1 influenza
before there was a vaccine for it in a surgical

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.140
mask.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:05.140
And I've taken care of patients with multidrug
resistant tuberculosis in an N95, and I have

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.280
trusted it to protect me and keep me healthy
and alive.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.320
And I still do to this day.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:19.230
But when the pandemic was bearing down on
us SARS-CoV-2, the COVID 19 pandemic, I started

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:25.050
to have to really look at all of that literature
because questions started to arise that were

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.310
really quite contentious.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:34.860
The first question was, should we be using
surgical masks or a 95 to take care of these

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.430
suspected COVID 19 patients?

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:43.470
That was a really difficult one because we
didn't have nearly enough and 95 available

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:48.860
in this country to take care of every person
with a respiratory infection in the winter

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.280
in an N95.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.390
And so we really needed to start looking at
the data, which suggested that it wasn't clear

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.840
cut, whether you needed an N95 in that situation.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.520
And the CDC was really kind of hedging on
its language.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.520
W.H.O.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.901
was being very clear that you did not need
an N95.

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:11.269
And so you may have seen that there was relatively
recently a clinical trial that assessed and

00:03:11.269 --> 00:03:12.950
95 versus surgical masks.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:17.010
They started back in those days when we really
didn't know what to do and didn't have enough

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:22.099
95 and actually found that they couldn't detect
a difference in health care worker infection

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:27.890
rates between those hospitals who used 95
for all of the care and those who used surgical

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.400
masks.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.260
That's not to say that the 95 aren't more
protective.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.909
Many of those healthcare workers got those
infections outside of health care.

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:39.170
Speaker 1: Right.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:45.159
And that seems to be a lot of the difficulty
where if we just say as sensible people, the

00:03:45.159 --> 00:03:50.390
tighter the barrier that we place between
ourselves and a pathogen seems pretty logical

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.220
that it reduces the chance of that pathogen
getting through.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.220
Great.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:55.439
Okay, so we can start there.

00:03:55.439 --> 00:03:59.019
But then you go to all sorts of different
studies and you have to contend with questions

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:03.500
like just because you have a mandate doesn't
mean everyone's doing it.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.189
Just because people are doing it part of the
day doesn't mean they're doing it all of the

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:08.189
day.

00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:11.849
Just because you are ostensibly wearing an
N95 mask doesn't mean you're wearing it correctly

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.070
or you don't have it pulled down or pulled
up or, you know, on your sleeve.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:22.570
So so are those some of the difficulties in
getting to what is the the true answer?

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.830
Speaker 3: That's exactly right.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:29.580
And that's really why the question is, is
more than do masks work?

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:37.810
It's do mask mandates work or does masking
on a community level result in a lower level

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:44.150
of infection transmission or number of infections
that not masking on a community level?

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.500
And what are you comparing it to?

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:46.940
No masks at all.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.860
Optional masking versus mandated masking.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.230
You know, all of those things are there.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.360
All of those comparisons are different comparisons.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:02.919
And we have data that show that there was
broader sort of community level interventions

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:10.380
are are quite a bit less effective than you
would want them to be, given that we do believe,

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:16.180
although it is really hard to show with science,
that masks are protecting us when we wear

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.470
them.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:23.360
And some of the data evidence for that was
also quite early on in health care.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.880
In looking specifically at SARS-CoV-2, what
we were seeing was that health care workers

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.610
did not have higher rates of SARS-CoV-2 antibody
positivity.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.780
People who weren't healthcare workers, even
though we were surrounded by COVID 19 patients

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:41.780
all day long.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:45.630
And that was telling me that our PPE was working.

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:52.419
Speaker 1: There's sort of a parallel, maybe
we would call it an issue here that is separate

00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:56.880
from a mask mandate, which maybe we can also
introduce, which is I've read a couple of

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:03.530
studies which suggests that when news media
is accurately informing people about the prevalence

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:10.099
of a virus, meaning people are aware of when
risk is higher versus when when risk is lower,

00:06:10.099 --> 00:06:16.630
that people choose to behave differently,
not only in terms of optional mask wearing

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:21.780
in places like grocery stores, but also in
terms of how social am I going to be, am I

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.470
going to go to sporting events, etc..

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:28.730
So this would be the idea that it's less about
mandating one thing or another, but that if

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:33.789
people perceive, oh, there's a new variant
like for example, after Thanksgiving of whichever

00:06:33.789 --> 00:06:38.849
year it was when Delta first came or whichever
one it was, when people perceive the risk

00:06:38.849 --> 00:06:43.680
is higher, they will modify their behavior
and that this is a way that we actually do

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.530
flatten the curve over a period of a couple
of months.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:51.610
And so it's less about mandating but more
about informing, and that many people will

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:55.729
actually make the choice that is logical anyway.

00:06:55.729 --> 00:06:59.300
Is that something that needs to be considered
when the effectiveness is measured?

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.680
Speaker 3: Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:08.860
So, you know, this gets to one of the CDC
studies that was really instrumental in the

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:14.229
development of their recommendations during
the current wave to shift to the highest quality

00:07:14.229 --> 00:07:19.910
mask you can wear instead of cloth masks,
unless that's the highest quality mask you

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:20.910
can tolerate.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:21.910
Yep.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:24.789
So commendation was in 95 for the most protective.

00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:25.789
Okay.

00:07:25.789 --> 00:07:29.220
And 95 of the second most protective surgical
masks and then cloth masks.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:33.730
And then that was based on this paper that
they put out.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:42.860
And there MMW are report showing that people
who used 95 or so people who tested positive

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:50.360
versus negative for SARS-CoV-2 infection,
COVID infection, where were more likely to

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:55.210
be negative if they had used a higher quality
mask, essentially was the result of that study.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:08:01.639
And the critique to that is people who wear
N95 are more likely to be COVID conscious

00:08:01.639 --> 00:08:02.740
in general.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:03.740
Right.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:07.069
Which is, you know, because those were not
that was not a randomized controlled clinical

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:08.069
trial.

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:16.789
And this is the critique for any observational
study, any epidemiologic study, any study

00:08:16.789 --> 00:08:21.789
where you compare to two geographical locations,
for example.

00:08:21.789 --> 00:08:27.600
So there's a new study that shows that, you
know, states and counties with mask mandates

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.820
had lower infection in the United States,
but not lower death rates, for example.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:35.769
Some of those things are exactly what you're
saying.

00:08:35.769 --> 00:08:41.960
If you don't randomize in a randomized controlled
clinical trial and you just observe the people

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:47.430
who choose to wear the the higher quality
mask, people who choose to wear a mask over,

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:52.190
no masks are also taking COVID more seriously
and doing those other things.

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:57.360
The states and counties that choose to issue
mandates are also issuing mandates related

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.360
to other things.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:02.860
They may have more business closures, they
may have more people in them that take COVID

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:03.860
seriously.

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:04.860
Right.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:05.860
That's fine.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:08.329
The Conference of Systematic Review old and
new.

00:09:08.329 --> 00:09:15.380
And really any time they do a systematic review
on anything, masks and treatments.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:20.410
In medicine, we'll always focus on randomized
controlled clinical trials because that's

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:23.750
the only way to take all those other factors
out of the calculation.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:28.380
Speaker 1: If we want to, in sort of one sentence,
give what might be the most comprehensive

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.350
but also accurate assessment.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:36.649
Is it fair to say that a correctly worn high
quality mask does a good job of protecting

00:09:36.649 --> 00:09:38.700
the individual wearing it?

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:44.399
But mask mandates are less effective at protecting
a population for all of the different reasons

00:09:44.399 --> 00:09:45.810
that you've cited so far.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.570
Like, is that kind of where we land?

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:52.470
Speaker 3: That's what I believe to be true.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:56.550
I can't say that we have great data.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:02.630
Prove that you wearing an N95 is definitely
going to protect you from COVID 19.

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:08.800
But that's what my three plus years of experience
in a hospital being bathed in COVID 19.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:09.800
Tell me a plus.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:10.800
The what?

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.040
I you know, what I referred to as the seroprevalence
data for health care workers, etc. the low

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.660
rate of transmission that I see on a day to
day basis.

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:21.079
And it's my job to see that between patients
and staff.

00:10:21.079 --> 00:10:26.630
So I believe that a high quality well-fitting
mask will protect me if I wear one.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:31.589
But I agree with the second part of what you
said, which is that I have been unable to

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:37.839
see convincing evidence that a mask mandate
or that community masking makes any difference

00:10:37.839 --> 00:10:40.440
in the incidence of infection.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:47.610
And the data that we have from 78 clinical
trials suggest that if there is a difference

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.920
there, it's very, very small.

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.250
The epidemiologic data comparing county to
county and state to state and country to country

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:59.639
based on mask use suggests that if there's
any difference there at all, it's very, very

00:10:59.639 --> 00:11:00.690
small.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:06.020
And that should really call into question
that the discussion around future mandates.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:10.250
In my opinion, even though, as I said, I've
been trusting masks with my life.

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:15.760
Speaker 1: There are all sorts of claims that
we've seen over the last few years about the

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:17.970
harms of masks.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:23.100
Sometimes these are what people call psychological
harms related to kids in schools or being

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.080
able to communicate or whatever the case may
be.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:30.970
You then have the stuff like, Well, it forces
you to re breathe your own carbon dioxide

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:32.639
and all of these different things.

00:11:32.639 --> 00:11:39.380
And they've to my relative satisfaction mostly
been all debunked with the exception of it

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:45.940
does seem that for little babies exposure
to an overwhelming number of people where

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:52.209
they don't see their mouth when they are being
spoken to may delay language development to

00:11:52.209 --> 00:11:53.500
some degree.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:54.990
And it seems to be temporary.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:59.740
I mean, I know I'm bringing a lot of new,
new things in here, But on the criticisms

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:02.430
of the masks, what do we actually know at
this point?

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:08.779
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, if we go back to
when we first started to think about community

00:12:08.779 --> 00:12:11.230
level masking, which was in health care.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:18.050
So it's March of 2020, more and more of our
health care workers are getting infected generally

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:22.590
at home and in the community, not through
care of patients with COVID because they're

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:25.019
wearing masks with patients.

00:12:25.019 --> 00:12:30.089
And we are and they're getting exposed and
we're having to furlough them for two weeks.

00:12:30.089 --> 00:12:33.320
We 5 minutes after we started to do that,
we realize we can't run a hospital with the

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:38.610
number of people that are home waiting to,
you know, usually not contract COVID.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:42.480
After an exposure, we start bringing them
back and we have them wear those were exposed

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:43.480
to COVID.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:44.480
We have to wear masks.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:48.730
So, you know, very quickly, half of our health
care workforce was wearing masks, but we didn't

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:56.029
have a mask mandate in health care yet because
masks had been shown in several meta analysis

00:12:56.029 --> 00:12:57.950
not to be effective for that purpose.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:12:59.709
And that's why we had avoided doing that.

00:12:59.709 --> 00:13:06.540
But as I watched people wearing masks because
of exposure, I saw a lot of people touching

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:11.800
them, you know, touching them, people putting
them down on the table to eat on a surface

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:13.959
that somebody else was going to eat on.

00:13:13.959 --> 00:13:17.980
I saw a lot of things that made me really
nervous about that.

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:24.130
Even the having the conversation around bringing
masks in as a long term strategy as a mandate

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:27.350
for health care workers.

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:28.350
But we did it.

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:34.630
We did it because, you know, it got to the
point where everyone was exposed and we needed

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:36.420
to protect each other.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:43.240
And then, as you know, it became standard
and recommended by the CDC for the whole country.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:48.160
You know, the data on its effectiveness hadn't
changed.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:53.149
The data on community masking hadn't changed
between the CDC not recommending community

00:13:53.149 --> 00:13:56.009
masking and recommending community masking.

00:13:56.009 --> 00:14:02.959
But the stakes were so high, people were dying
and the hospitals were filling up and people

00:14:02.959 --> 00:14:06.610
were going to be dying of non-covid reasons
because they couldn't get a bed in the hospital

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:07.610
and it was worth it.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.779
It was worth it to try.

00:14:09.779 --> 00:14:15.600
And I have to admit, I didn't have the creativity
to think how we could possibly be implement

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:19.269
community masking on a national level given
the supply chain issues.

00:14:19.269 --> 00:14:24.200
I didn't think that people could make them
at home and I lacked that creativity and I

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:25.220
was wrong.

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:28.050
We did it and we did it effectively.

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:33.920
But then what I started to see over time,
especially as the children in schools were

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:41.269
wearing masks, was but also here in the hospital,
working every day throughout the whole pandemic

00:14:41.269 --> 00:14:44.259
was a loss of connection.

00:14:44.259 --> 00:14:48.630
When you couldn't see people's expressions
in people's faces, you were just much less

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:49.630
like.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:54.290
To have those kind of intimate conversations.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:15:02.860
We were able to drop masks in the hospital
in areas where patients and the public were

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:07.440
restricted from entering, so after everyone
was vaccinated.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:12.980
In the summer of 2021, we stopped wearing
masks in offices, in conference rooms and

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:13.980
break rooms.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:22.950
And it was night and day in terms of morale,
combating burnout and what we had just been

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:23.950
through.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:28.740
And then we had to put the masks back on because
the Delta variant was immune, evasive, and

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:29.759
people were vaccinated.

00:15:29.759 --> 00:15:31.220
People were getting infected.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:32.220
Yeah.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:33.680
And it was hard.

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:38.880
So I think that sure, there are there are
there are definite downsides when it comes

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.410
to people who are hearing impaired, not being
able to tell what's going on, people who are

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:50.430
trying to learn language, people with speech
impairments, you know, and children who have

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:54.959
developmental delays and that sort of social
interaction piece.

00:15:54.959 --> 00:16:02.180
But I, I really did see amongst my my children
and my friends children and amongst my colleagues

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:09.090
just an impact on connection, mood, morale
that has really improved.

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:12.260
Again, when we were able to take masks off
about a year ago.

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:16.980
Speaker 1: And last thing, the re breathing
of your own carbon dioxide does not seem to

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:17.980
be a problem.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.050
Speaker 3: No, no, no.

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:21.459
I was sorry about that at all.

00:16:21.459 --> 00:16:25.910
And you know that we do know that that our
surgeons wear masks for our cases.

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:28.180
And, you know, there's there's there's no
issue.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:31.130
So that would not be one of my concerns now.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:37.980
And 95, you know, OSHA does require that that
workers be medically cleared to wear and 95

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:43.199
respirators, those will cause shortness of
breath that can cause, you know, cardiovascular

00:16:43.199 --> 00:16:45.450
issues when you wear them for prolonged periods
of time.

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:49.900
And, of course, we've all seen the pictures
of the skin breakdown that you can have if

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.130
you have to wear them for a prolonged period
of periods of time, during a period where

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:57.649
everybody has COVID or suspected COVID, which
was the case in spring of 2020.

00:16:57.649 --> 00:16:59.649
So those can have some health implications.

00:16:59.649 --> 00:17:01.089
Speaker 1: All right.

